{
 "id": "cor-a1-fourier-middle-hc1",
 "blueprint": {
  "is": "cor-a1-fourier-middle-hc1",
  "from": null,
  "same_as": null
 },
 "kind": "statement",
 "title": "Fourier bound for middle-extension cohomology",
 "statement": "Let $j:U_0\\hookrightarrow\\mathbf A^1_{k_0}$ be a dense open immersion and\nlet $\\mathcal M$ be an irreducible\nlisse Weil sheaf on\n$U_0$, unramified at infinity,\npunctually $\\iota$-pure of weight\n$w$, with irreducible geometric fundamental representation of\n[def-wf-fundamental-representation] and\nFourier-nonexceptional\nmiddle extension\n$j_*\\mathcal M$. Then\n$H_c^1(\\mathbf A^1_{k_0},j_*\\mathcal M)$ has\nweights at most $w+1$.",
 "source": {
  "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-deligne-laumon-a1/sk-c-dl-a1-hc1/cor-a1-fourier-middle-hc1.md",
  "place": "node cor-a1-fourier-middle-hc1, order 2782"
 },
 "stmt_deps": [
  "def-wf-fundamental-representation"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "*Step 1: the transform of the middle extension.* The hypothesis\n[def-fd-nonexceptional] excludes, after geometric base change, a\nconstant sheaf up to a constant Weil twist; so $\\mathcal M$ is not\ngeometrically constant. Being nonzero, irreducible and unramified at\ninfinity, $\\mathcal M$ is therefore constituent-nonexceptional by\n[lem-a1-unramified-nonexceptional], and\n[prop-a1-fourier-middle-extension] supplies a dense open\n$j':U'_0\\hookrightarrow\\mathbf A^1_{k_0}$ of the dual line and a nonzero\nirreducible lisse Weil sheaf $\\mathcal M'$ on $U'_0$ with\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{FT}_\\psi\\bigl((j_*\\mathcal M)[1]\\bigr)\\cong(j'_*\\mathcal M')[1].\n$$",
   "uses": [
    "def-fd-nonexceptional",
    "lem-a1-unramified-nonexceptional",
    "prop-a1-fourier-middle-extension"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  },
  {
   "text": "*Step 2: the weight of $\\mathcal M'$.* Since $\\mathcal M$ is moreover\npunctually $\\iota$-pure of weight $w$, the last assertion of\n[cor-fd-l2-plancherel-middle-extension] shows that $\\mathcal M'$ is\npunctually $\\iota$-pure of weight exactly $w+1$ on all of $U'_0$.",
   "uses": [
    "cor-fd-l2-plancherel-middle-extension"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  },
  {
   "text": "*Step 3: weights of the middle extension on the dual line.* The dual\nline is a smooth geometrically connected affine curve, and\n$\\mathcal M'$ is a nonzero lisse Weil sheaf on the dense open $U'_0$\nall of whose closed-point Frobenius eigenvalues satisfy\n$|\\iota(\\alpha)|^2=q_x^{w+1}\\le q_x^{w+1}$. Boundary semicontinuity\n[lem-wf-boundary-semicontinuity], with $\\beta=w+1$, shows that the\nmiddle extension $j'_*\\mathcal M'$ of [def-wf-middle-extension] has\npunctual weights at most $w+1$ at every closed point of the dual line,\nincluding the finitely many points outside $U'_0$, where it may be\nsingular.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-wf-boundary-semicontinuity",
    "def-wf-middle-extension"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  },
  {
   "text": "*Step 4: the origin fibre.* By the stationary-phase identification\n[cor-fd-origin-stationary-phase], whose hypotheses are those of the\npresent statement, there is a Frobenius-equivariant isomorphism\n\n$$\nH_c^1(\\mathbf A^1_{k_0},j_*\\mathcal M)\\cong(j'_*\\mathcal M')_{0'},\n$$\n\nthe geometric Frobenius of $k_0$ on the left corresponding to the local\nFrobenius $F_{0'}$ at the $k_0$-rational point $0'$ on the right. Hence\nthe eigenvalues of Frobenius on $H_c^1(\\mathbf A^1_{k_0},j_*\\mathcal M)$\nare those of $F_{0'}$ on the stalk, and by Step 3 (with $q_{0'}=q$)\neach of them satisfies $|\\iota(\\alpha)|^2\\le q^{w+1}$. In the\nconvention of [def-weil-punctual-weights], this says that\n$H_c^1(\\mathbf A^1_{k_0},j_*\\mathcal M)$ has weights at most $w+1$.\nThe irreducibility of the geometric fundamental representation of\n[def-wf-fundamental-representation] is not needed for this argument.",
   "uses": [
    "cor-fd-origin-stationary-phase",
    "def-weil-punctual-weights",
    "def-wf-fundamental-representation"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  }
 ],
